></span>.<p>Alternate approach: Because <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"a, plus b, plus c\"></span> is the value of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"a, x squared, plus b x, plus c\"></span> when <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x equals 1\"></span>, it is possible to first make that substitution into each polynomial before adding them. When <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x equals 1\"></span>, the first polynomial is equal to<span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"negative 2 plus 1, plus 31, equals 30\"></span> and the second polynomial is equal to <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"3 plus 7, minus 8, equals 2\"></span>. The sum of 30 and 2 is 32.</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959636,"updateDate":1691007959636,"correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale":true}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959636, 1691007959636),
    ($SATQ$f25a34aa$SATQ$, $SATQ$6e51407b-255f-4375-91cf-b3145e516d19$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Advanced Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$P$SATQ$, $SATQ$P.C.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Nonlinear functions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The area of a triangle is equal to <math alttext="x squared"><msup>
	<mi>x</mi>
	<mn>2</mn>
</msup>
</math> square centimeters. The length of the base of the triangle is <math alttext="2 x plus 22"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mn>2</mn>
		<mi>x</mi>
	</mrow>
	<mo>+</mo>
	<mn>22</mn>
</mrow>
</math> centimeters, and the height of the triangle is <math alttext="x minus 10"><mrow>
	<mi>x</mi>
	<mo>-</mo>
	<mn>10</mn>
</mrow>
</math> centimeters. What is the value of <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["110"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["110"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="110"><mn>110</mn></math>. The area of a triangle is equal to one half of the product of the length of the base of the triangle and the height of the triangle. It's given that the length of the base of the triangle is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="2 x plus 22"><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>22</mn></math> centimeters and the height of the triangle is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x minus 10"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></math> centimeters; therefore, its area is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="one half left parenthesis 2 x plus 22 right parenthesis left parenthesis x minus 10 right parenthesis"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>)</mo></math> square centimeters. It's also given that the area of the triangle is equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x squared"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> square centimeters. Therefore, it follows that <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="one half left parenthesis 2 x plus 22 right parenthesis left parenthesis x minus 10 right parenthesis equals x squared"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. &nbsp;This equation can be rewritten as <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis x plus 11 right parenthesis left parenthesis x minus 10 right parenthesis equals x squared"><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x squared plus x minus 110 equals x squared"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>110</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Subtracting <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x squared"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> from both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x minus 110 equals 0"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>110</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. Adding&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="110"><mn>110</mn></math> to both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x equals 110"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>110</mn></math>. Therefore, the value of&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x"><mi>x</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="110"><mn>110</mn></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f25a34aa","external_id":"6e51407b-255f-4375-91cf-b3145e516d19","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"f08a3f45-368e-42da-bf5d-e480cb8c2c82","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"P","domain":"Advanced Math","skill_cd":"P.C.","skill_desc":"Nonlinear functions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The area of a triangle is equal to <math alttext=\"x squared\"><msup>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n</msup>\n</math> square centimeters. The length of the base of the triangle is <math alttext=\"2 x plus 22\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mn>22</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math> centimeters, and the height of the triangle is <math alttext=\"x minus 10\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t<mn>10</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math> centimeters. What is the value of <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math>?</p>","answerOptions":null,"keys":["110"],"correct_answer":["110"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"110\"><mn>110</mn></math>. The area of a triangle is equal to one half of the product of the length of the base of the triangle and the height of the triangle. It's given that the length of the base of the triangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2 x plus 22\"><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>22</mn></math> centimeters and the height of the triangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x minus 10\"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></math> centimeters; therefore, its area is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"one half left parenthesis 2 x plus 22 right parenthesis left parenthesis x minus 10 right parenthesis\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>)</mo></math> square centimeters. It's also given that the area of the triangle is equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x squared\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> square centimeters. Therefore, it follows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"one half left parenthesis 2 x plus 22 right parenthesis left parenthesis x minus 10 right parenthesis equals x squared\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. &nbsp;This equation can be rewritten as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis x plus 11 right parenthesis left parenthesis x minus 10 right parenthesis equals x squared\"><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x squared plus x minus 110 equals x squared\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>110</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Subtracting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x squared\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> from both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x minus 110 equals 0\"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>110</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. Adding&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"110\"><mn>110</mn></math> to both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals 110\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>110</mn></math>. Therefore, the value of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"110\"><mn>110</mn></math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1755117017719,"pPcc":"SAT#P","questionId":"f25a34aa","skill_cd":"P.C.","score_band_range_cd":7,"skill_desc":"Nonlinear functions","createDate":1755117017719,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Advanced Math","ibn":"","external_id":"6e51407b-255f-4375-91cf-b3145e516d19","primary_class_cd":"P","uId":"f08a3f45-368e-42da-bf5d-e480cb8c2c82","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"type":"spr","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The area of a triangle is equal to <math alttext=\"x squared\"><msup>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n</msup>\n</math> square centimeters. The length of the base of the triangle is <math alttext=\"2 x plus 22\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mn>22</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math> centimeters, and the height of the triangle is <math alttext=\"x minus 10\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t<mn>10</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math> centimeters. What is the value of <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math>?</p>","keys":["110"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"110\"><mn>110</mn></math>. The area of a triangle is equal to one half of the product of the length of the base of the triangle and the height of the triangle. It's given that the length of the base of the triangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2 x plus 22\"><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>22</mn></math> centimeters and the height of the triangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x minus 10\"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></math> centimeters; therefore, its area is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"one half left parenthesis 2 x plus 22 right parenthesis left parenthesis x minus 10 right parenthesis\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>)</mo></math> square centimeters. It's also given that the area of the triangle is equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x squared\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> square centimeters. Therefore, it follows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"one half left parenthesis 2 x plus 22 right parenthesis left parenthesis x minus 10 right parenthesis equals x squared\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. &nbsp;This equation can be rewritten as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis x plus 11 right parenthesis left parenthesis x minus 10 right parenthesis equals x squared\"><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x squared plus x minus 110 equals x squared\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>110</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Subtracting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x squared\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> from both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x minus 110 equals 0\"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>110</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. Adding&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"110\"><mn>110</mn></math> to both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals 110\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>110</mn></math>. Therefore, the value of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"110\"><mn>110</mn></math>.</p>","externalid":"6e51407b-255f-4375-91cf-b3145e516d19","correct_answer":["110"]},"createDate":1755117017719,"updateDate":1755117017719}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117017719, 1755117017719),
